            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> Nice, thanks! Does this close https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/39438 ? \n\nNot directly. Document link is still just a method in buf.lua, and this doesn't do anything about unifying the way the decoration providers work or extmark control across features.\n\n\n> Anything we should say about this in news.txt?\n\nMaybe just a statement about inlay hints generally being more stable and less buggy? Not sure exactly.\n\n\n> Looks like a lateral move in terms of code cost, but fixes a lot and centralizes stuff in the Capability framework.\n\nIt was actually much smaller (more lines removed by quite a bit) when it was just a straight refactor, but then I started adding more comments and fixing those bugs 😂\n\n> btw, are you noticing any opportunities to improve the Capability abstraction? Ideally it would result in each feature requiring less code.\n\nGenerally they do require much less code and also forces each feature to keep client state separate from other client state per buffer and forces a general lifecycle of that state so it's easier to reason about across them. All these features now have largely the same shape and patterns, which will be great for maintainability.\n\nFor more abstraction, yes I think there are things we can do. The issue you linked has an idea for one. I've also been thinking about a way to make the common capability class handle the `LspNotify` autocmd itself and just using a couple callbacks (`on_document_close()`,  `on_document_open()`, and `on_document_change()`, or `reset()` and `send_request()`) to all enabled features for the buffer when it fires. This would reduce the count of those autocmds to one per buffer instead of one per buffer per feature that uses it and make it harder to get wrong.\n\nDoing too much additional abstraction runs the risk of making it harder to do the \"different\"/\"unique\" things they each need. The decoration provider abstraction is a neat idea, but as mentioned in the linked issue, the _way_ these features all apply extmarks is pretty different. We could do the `on_win()` abstraction and register one \"capability\" decorator where each attached capability gets the on_win callback, that would save a few things in semantic tokens and inlay hints.\n\nI'll keep thinking about it, but those are my thoughts so far. ",
